<exercisegroup cols="2">
<title>Guessing inversion patterns from multiplication patterns</title>

<introduction><p>
	In each of the following,
	use the referenced multiplication pattern from <xref ref="exercises-matrix-ops" /> to answer the following about the given matrix.
	<ol>
		<li>
			Using the idea that an inverse should <q>reverse</q> multiplication,
			guess the inverse of matrix.
		</li>
		<li> Verify that your guess is correct by multiplying it against the original matrix. </li>
		<li>
			Determine whether there any values or combinations of values for the variable entries in the original matrix for which your inverse doesn't work.
			In those situations, is the original matrix invertible?
		</li>
	</ol>
</p></introduction>

<exercise><p>
	Multiplication pattern from <xref ref="exercise-matrix-ops-mult-diag" />;
	matrix
	<me>
		\begin{bmatrix}
			a \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
			0 \amp b \amp 0 \\
			0 \amp 0 \amp c
		\end{bmatrix}
	</me>.
</p></exercise>

<exercise><p>
	Multiplication pattern from <xref ref="exercise-matrix-ops-patterns-of-unipotent-isolates-addition" />;
	matrix
	<me>
		\begin{bmatrix}
			1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
			0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp a \\
			0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
			0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1
		\end{bmatrix}
	</me>.
</p></exercise>

</exercisegroup>
